export default "## What \"Cramps But No Period\" Actually Means\n\nCramping is a signal your body uses for multiple different processes, not only menstruation. The pelvic region contains the uterus, ovaries, fallopian tubes, bladder, and lower segments of the bowel. Cramping can come from any of these structures, which is why the symptom alone is not diagnostic.\n\nThe most useful way to approach cramping without a period is to figure out where you are in your cycle, what the pain feels like, and whether other symptoms are present. Those three pieces of information together narrow the possibilities substantially.\n\nIf bladder pain, urgent peeing, or burning when peeing is part of the pattern, use the [bladder pain before period tracker](/free/period-pain-cramp-diary) or the [UTI during period symptom log](/free/uti-urinary-symptom-tracker) to bring cleaner notes to care.\n\nIf bowel changes are part of the pattern, use the [period symptom tracker](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker) or the [period cramp and pain diary](/free/period-pain-cramp-diary) to keep stool and pain details separate from the pelvic pain story.\n\nIf pain location is the main question, use the [period pain location visit summary](/free/period-pain-cramp-diary) to keep back, leg, rectal, bladder, and belly notes in one short handoff.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only and is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## Ovulation Pain (Mittelschmerz)\n\nIf your cramps arrive around the middle of your cycle (roughly two weeks before your expected period), ovulation may be one explanation. When an ovary releases an egg, the follicle ruptures. That process can cause a small amount of fluid or blood to irritate the pelvic lining, producing brief, one-sided pain.\n\nMittelschmerz is typically:\n- One-sided, on the side of the ovary that ovulated (which can switch cycle to cycle)\n- Brief, lasting from a few minutes to a few hours, occasionally up to two days\n- Accompanied by a small amount of mid-cycle spotting in some people\n- Consistent in pattern across cycles\n\nIf mid-cycle pain matches this description and has appeared reliably across multiple cycles at the same approximate time, ovulation pain is one possibility to discuss if the pain is not severe or changing.\n\n## Late Luteal Phase Cramping Before Period Arrives\n\nThe days just before a period can involve cramping that feels like the period has arrived, only for menstruation to start hours or days later. This happens because prostaglandins (the compounds that trigger uterine contractions) begin ramping up as progesterone drops in the late luteal phase. The uterus starts preparing to shed its lining before visible bleeding begins.\n\nThis is one possible explanation for \"cramps but no period\" in people with regular cycles. If your period follows within a day or two and symptoms are not severe or unusual for you, cycle timing may be part of the pattern.\n\n## Delayed or Irregular Cycles\n\nIf your period is late, cramping can have more than one explanation. Stress, illness, significant weight changes, overtraining, and sleep disruption can affect ovulation timing, which can affect when a period arrives.\n\nTracking cycle length over several months reveals whether irregularity is a consistent pattern or an occasional deviation. See the guide on tracking irregular cycles for more detail.\n\n## Ovarian Cysts\n\nOvarian cysts form as a normal part of the ovulation process. The follicle that releases an egg is technically a cyst that typically resolves on its own. When a cyst persists, grows larger, or ruptures, it can cause pelvic pain ranging from a dull ache to sharp, sudden cramping.\n\nSigns that point toward a cyst rather than normal cycle pain:\n- Pain that persists throughout the cycle, not just at ovulation or before menstruation\n- Bloating on one side of the lower abdomen\n- Discomfort during sex\n- A dull, consistent ache rather than wave-like cramping\n\nMany functional ovarian cysts resolve without treatment. Large cysts, complex cysts (those that are not entirely fluid-filled), or cysts causing significant pain are evaluated with ultrasound by a clinician.\n\nIf an ovarian cyst is part of the concern, use the [ovarian cyst symptom checklist](/free/ovarian-cyst-tracking-kit). If bleeding or timing changed too, use the [ovarian cyst period change tracker](/free/ovarian-cyst-tracking-kit).\n\n## Endometriosis\n\nEndometriosis (a condition where tissue similar to the uterine lining grows outside the uterus) causes pelvic pain that is not limited to menstruation. Endometriosis tissue responds to hormonal cycles, swelling, bleeding, and irritating surrounding structures throughout the cycle. Pain during ovulation, pain in the luteal phase, and pain entirely separate from the menstrual phase are all characteristic.\n\nIf you experience consistent pelvic cramping across multiple phases of the cycle (particularly if combined with painful periods, pain with bowel movements or urination around menstruation, or pain during sex), endometriosis is worth raising with a clinician.\n\n## Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID)\n\nPID is an infection of the reproductive organs (often caused by STIs that were not treated) that produces persistent pelvic pain. Unlike cycle-related cramping, PID pain does not follow a predictable cycle pattern. It may be continuous, worsen with movement, and come with fever, unusual discharge, and pain during sex.\n\nPID is treatable with antibiotics. Early treatment matters significantly for long-term reproductive health.\n\n## Ectopic Pregnancy: The One That Cannot Wait\n\nIf you could be pregnant and you are experiencing one-sided pelvic pain, get urgent medical care. Ectopic pregnancy is one serious possibility a clinician may need to check.\n\nAn ectopic pregnancy implants in a fallopian tube rather than the uterus. As it grows, it puts pressure on the tube and can cause it to rupture, which is a surgical emergency. Symptoms include:\n- One-sided pelvic pain, sometimes radiating to the shoulder\n- Vaginal bleeding (though not always present)\n- A positive pregnancy test\n- Dizziness or feeling faint if rupture has occurred\n\nIf this cluster of symptoms is present: go to an emergency room. This is not a wait-and-see situation.\n\n## Gastrointestinal Causes\n\nThe bowel sits near the reproductive organs. Gas pain, constipation cramping, and IBS flares can feel like period cramps. Digestive cramping may show up with bowel changes or ease after passing gas or having a bowel movement.\n\nCycle timing can make the pattern feel linked, but timing alone does not prove the cause. Track bowel symptoms beside pelvic pain so a clinician can decide what to check.\n\n## What to Track\n\nWhen cramping occurs without a period:\n\n- **Date and cycle day:** count from your last period start date\n- **Location:** central, left side, right side, lower back, radiating anywhere\n- **Character:** dull/aching, sharp, cramping/wave-like, constant\n- **Intensity:** 1 to 10 scale\n- **Duration:** how long each episode lasts and how many days total\n- **Associated symptoms:** nausea, fever, unusual discharge, bowel changes, urinary symptoms, spotting\n- **Pregnancy status:** whether you have taken a test and the result\n\nFor non-urgent symptoms, a log covering two to three cycles gives a clinician a much clearer starting point than a verbal description from memory.\n\nIf the notes include many body locations, the [period pain location data privacy checklist](/resources/privacy-in-practice/protect-your-symptom-and-condition-data) can help you choose what to keep, export, or leave out.\n\n## The Privacy Issue With Tracking This Data\n\nCramping, cycle irregularity, and spotting data, especially when combined with pregnancy test results, can be sensitive reproductive health information. Depending on where you live and who holds the record, health data may be exposed through legal requests, breaches, account access, screenshots, exports, or backups.\n\nFlo Health's 2021 FTC settlement and later class action settlement showed how one major period tracking app shared user data with third parties without enough disclosure. Many consumer period apps are not covered by HIPAA. Coverage depends on who holds the data and why.\n\nOn-device tracking can reduce the company-held records tied to your symptoms. It does not remove every risk. Screenshots, exports, phone backups, shared devices, and your own choices about what to send or show can still expose sensitive details.";
